 ## Overview
-Vector Topology Analysis is a technique that analyzes the global structure of a vector field by identifying and classifying critical points—locations where the vector magnitude is zero [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from advection-based methods like Stream Lines by focusing on the geometric singularities of the field rather than continuous trajectories, providing a compact "topology skeleton" that captures the essential flow features [S1, unofficial]. Unlike scalar field topology methods, it operates directly on the vector components to determine flow behavior near stagnation points [S2, unofficial].
+Vector Topology Analysis is a technique to analyze vector field topology based on critical point classification to capture global features of a flow field [S1, unofficial]. Critical points are defined as points in a vector field where the vector is zero [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from advection-based methods like Stream Lines by focusing on geometric singularities rather than continuous trajectories, producing a compact topology skeleton [S1, unofficial]. Unlike scalar field topology methods, it operates directly on vector components to determine flow behavior near stagnation points [G].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A discrete vector field $\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x})$ defined on a grid or mesh.
-**Outputs:** A set of classified critical points and a topology skeleton (streamlines connecting them).
